// A `match` that falls through, INSIDE a `try_or`. The subtlest of the three
// things the four backends disagreed about, and the one no harness could see.
//
// `uncaught_nonexhaustive.mere` next door pins the message and the status. This
// pins CATCHABILITY, which is a different property and was wrong in a different
// way on each backend before v0.1.470: the interpreter handed back the try_or
// default, the C and LLVM backends were killed by a bare `abort()` (a signal is
// not something a jmpbuf sees), Wasm trapped on `unreachable`, and RV32IM never
// failed at all — it returned the saved stack pointer as the match's value and
// the try_or had nothing to catch.
//
// So this file is here rather than in fail/ because it SUCCEEDS: it is the shape
// where the answer differs while every backend exits 0, which the failure
// section cannot compare. It is also in the corpus `scripts/rv_exec_check.sh`
// sweeps, which is the only gate that runs RV32IM output — the failure section
// covers four backends and RV is not one of them.
//
// The vehicle is an int scrutinee for the reason written next door: a missing
// case the checker can name is a compile error, so the only fallthrough a
// compiling program can reach is one the checker admits it cannot name.

let name = fn (n: int) ->
  match n with
  | 0 -> "zero"
  | 1 -> "one";

let safe = fn (n: int) -> try_or (fn () -> name n) "caught";

let _ = print (name 1);
let _ = print (safe 0);
let _ = print (safe 7);
let _ = print (safe 1);
print (safe 99)
